1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Real-time and batching sound similar, but they are different territory.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
One answers you right now, at full price.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
The other runs in the background at half the cost, on its own schedule.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Knowing which one a job needs is the first call you can make to save on tokens.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,000
This lesson maps the line and explains the differences between the two. Let's dive in.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Let's start with the two surfaces themselves.

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
The Synchronous Messages API is the one you already know.

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,000
You send a request, your code blocks, and Claude sends back the response.

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
It's built for interactive work, and it's the only surface that streams tokens as they generate.

10
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,000
The Message Batches API works completely differently.

11
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Instead of one request at a time, you bundle many requests into a single job and submit it.

12
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Claude processes that job asynchronously in the background and hands everything back when it's done.

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,000
The headline difference is price.

14
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Batch work bills at 50% of the standard rate, both input and output.

15
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,000
You trade an instant answer for a serious discount.

16
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Three fundamentals define how a batch works.

17
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,000
First, the endpoint.

18
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,000
You post your job to the batch's endpoint, and each entry inside pairs a custom ID with params,

19
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,000
which is just a normal messages request body.

20
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,000
So every item in the batch is a full request you could have sent on its own.

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Second, the price, that half-rate discount you just met, which is the entire reason the batch's API exists.

22
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,000
Third, it's asynchronous.

23
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,000
You don't sit and wait on the call.

24
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:40,000
You submit the job, poll to see when it's finished, and then retrieve all the results together.

25
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Endpoint, price, asynchronous.

26
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Those three frame everything else.

27
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Let's sit on that cost tradeoff for a second, because it's the whole reason you'd choose a batch.

28
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Both your input tokens and your output tokens bill at half price.

29
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
On a handful of requests, that's pocket change.

30
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,000
On a job with tens of thousands of them, it's real money saved.

31
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,000
But nothing's free.

32
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,000
In exchange for that discount, you give up a couple of things you'd get from the synchronous API.

33
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
You don't get an immediate response, and you can't stream.

34
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,000
For anything a person is watching in real time, that's a deal-breaker.

35
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:23,000
For a large offline job that runs overnight, giving up speed to half the bill is usually an easy call.

36
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Timing is where batches trip people up, so let's get it exact. First, speed.

37
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Most batches finish in under an hour.

38
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
That's great, but it's best effort.

39
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Not a guaranteed service level, so never build a hard deadline on it.

40
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Second, the processing window.

41
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,000
A batch has to complete within 24 hours.

42
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Any request that doesn't finish inside that window expires, and expired requests are unbilled, so

43
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
you're not charged for them.

44
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Third, when results land.

45
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:01,000
They become available the moment every request completes, or at the 24-hour mark, whichever comes first.

46
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:08,000
So plan for up to a day of latency, and treat the under-an-hour case as a bonus, not a promise.

47
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Here's a distinction developers routinely blur.

48
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:15,000
There are two different windows, and they do completely different jobs.

49
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
The processing window is 24 hours.

50
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
That's the deadline for the job to finish.

51
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Miss it, and unprocessed requests expire, unbilled.

52
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
It decides whether your batch completes at all.

53
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
The retention window is 29 days.

54
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:37,000
That's how long your finished results stay downloadable, counting from when you created the batch.

55
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:42,000
After 29 days, the batch is still viewable, but you can't pull the results anymore.

56
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
So one window is about getting the work done, and the other is about how long you have to collect it.

57
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Don't let them collapse into one number.

58
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,000
A couple of hard ceilings shape how big a batch can get.

59
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:04,000
A single batch holds up to 100,000 requests, or 256 megabytes of total payload, whichever you hit first.

60
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,000
If your job is bigger than that, you split it across multiple batches.

61
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,000
There's one more constraint worth flagging here, and it's about data policy, not size.

62
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Batches aren't eligible for zero data retention.

63
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:22,000
So, if your organization requires that stricter retention posture, the Batches API is off the table,

64
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,000
and you'd run that work synchronously instead.

65
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Keep both facts in mind when you're sizing a job, because they can force your hand on architecture.

66
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Let's turn the two surfaces into an actual decision, starting with real-time.

67
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,000
You stay on the Synchronous Messages API whenever speed is the requirement.

68
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:48,000
The clearest signal is a user waiting on the response right now, a chat reply, a live coding

69
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
assistant, anything interactive.

70
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:58,000
It's also your only option when you want to stream tokens as they generate, since batches can't stream at all.

71
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:05,000
And more broadly, any latency-bound work, where the time to answer is part of the product, belongs here.

72
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:11,000
The simple test is this. If a person or a downstream system is blocked, waiting on that answer, you

73
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:12,000
send it in real-time.

74
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Don't batch something someone is watching.

75
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:21,000
Batches are the flip side. You reach for them when volume matters more than immediacy.

76
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:27,000
The classic fits are large offline workloads, running an eval suite across thousands of test cases,

77
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:34,000
bulk-classifying a giant backlog, or processing a pile of documents where nobody is waiting on any single result.

78
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:41,000
If the work is latency-insensitive, and there's a lot of it, that 50% discount adds up fast.

79
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,000
One caution, because people conflate these.

80
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Firing off many parallel real-time calls isn't the same thing as batching.

81
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
That's still full price, and it leans on your rate limits.

82
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:59,000
The Batches API is a distinct, discounted, asynchronous job, and that's the tool for genuine bulk work.

83
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:06,000
Let's recap. Real-time and batching aren't one API with a toggle. They're different tools.

84
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:12,000
The Synchronous Messages API answers immediately, streams, and bills at full price.

85
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:18,000
The Message Batches API runs asynchronously at half price with no streaming.

86
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:23,000
The implication is simple. Match the surface to the latency the work can tolerate.

87
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:28,000
That covers when to choose real-time versus batch. I'll see you in the next one.

